  { q: 'What is the Best Answer Hub Base64 Encoder and Decoder?', a: 'The Best Answer Hub Base64 Encoder and Decoder is a free, browser-based tool that converts text to Base64 and decodes Base64 back to text. It supports full Unicode, including emoji and non-Latin scripts, and runs entirely on your device using standard browser functions, so nothing is uploaded and there is no signup.' },
  { q: 'Is Base64 encryption?', a: 'No. Base64 is an encoding, not encryption. It uses no key and is a fixed, public transformation that anyone can reverse in one step. Encryption uses a secret key to keep data unreadable without it. Base64 only changes how data is represented, so it provides no confidentiality at all.' },
  { q: 'Can I use Base64 to hide a password or secret?', a: 'No, and it is a known security mistake. The weakness catalog CWE-261 states that obscuring a password with Base64 does not protect it, because anyone can decode it instantly. Use real encryption or a secrets manager for anything that must stay private. Base64 is for transport, never for secrecy.' },
  { q: 'What is Base64 actually used for?', a: 'Moving binary data through channels that only handle text. It embeds images in HTML or CSS via data URLs, carries email attachments through the MIME standard, encodes HTTP Basic Auth headers, and encodes the readable parts of a JWT. In each case the goal is safe transport, not keeping the content secret.' },
  { q: 'Why does Base64 make data about a third larger?', a: 'Because it represents every 3 bytes of input as 4 text characters, which adds roughly 33 percent to the size. That is the trade for a string that survives text-only systems. It is why Base64 suits small assets and short values, and why inlining a large image as a data URL can bloat a page.' },
  { q: 'What is the difference between Base64 and base64url?', a: 'base64url is a URL-safe variant defined in RFC 4648 that replaces the plus with a minus and the slash with an underscore, and usually drops the equals padding. It is used in JWTs and OAuth parameters. The Best Answer Hub Base64 Encoder outputs standard Base64, and the JWT Decoder reads base64url segments.' },
  { q: 'Why do some tools turn my emoji into garbage?', a: 'Because they use the browser btoa function, or the deprecated escape function, which assume every character is a single byte and cannot handle Unicode. The Best Answer Hub Base64 Encoder pairs btoa and atob with the modern TextEncoder and TextDecoder, so emoji and non-Latin scripts round-trip exactly with no mojibake.' },
  { q: 'Is it safe to paste a token or key into an online Base64 tool?', a: 'Only if the tool runs on your device. Many converters send what you paste to a server, which hands a token or key to a third party in fully readable form, since Base64 adds no protection. The Best Answer Hub Base64 Encoder processes everything locally, so nothing is transmitted.' },
  { q: 'How do I encode text to Base64?', a: 'Paste your text into the input area of the Best Answer Hub Base64 Encoder and click Encode. The tool converts it to a Base64 string using UTF-8 and shows the result, ready to copy. For example, the text Hello World becomes SGVsbG8gV29ybGQ equals. The whole conversion happens in your browser.' },
  { q: 'How do I decode Base64 back to text?', a: 'Paste the Base64 string into the Best Answer Hub Base64 Encoder and click Decode. The tool validates the string, adds padding if needed, and converts it back to readable text using UTF-8. If the input is not valid Base64, it shows a clear error rather than returning garbled characters.' },
  { q: 'Why does a Base64 string end with one or two equals signs?', a: 'Because Base64 works in groups of 4 characters, and the equals sign is padding used when the input does not divide evenly into 3 bytes. One trailing byte produces two equals signs, and two trailing bytes produce one. The base64url variant used in tokens usually omits this padding entirely.' },
  { q: 'Can anyone read a JWT because it is Base64?', a: 'Yes, for a standard signed token. A JWT header and payload are base64url-encoded, not encrypted, so anyone can decode and read the claims. Signing only proves the token was not tampered with, not that it is private. Never put a secret in a JWT payload unless the token itself is encrypted.' },
  { q: 'Does the Base64 tool work offline?', a: 'Yes. The Best Answer Hub Base64 Encoder is built with the browser own functions and needs no server, so after the page loads once it works with no internet connection. That makes it dependable for sensitive data in restricted or air-gapped environments, where nothing should be sent out.' },
  { q: 'How is this different from the browser btoa and atob functions?', a: 'The raw btoa and atob functions only handle single-byte characters and corrupt emoji or non-ASCII text. The Best Answer Hub Base64 Encoder wraps them with TextEncoder and TextDecoder for full Unicode support, and adds copy, clear, and clear error messages. For plain ASCII, btoa works fine, but for real-world text this is safer.' },
